[00:09.40]This week, Paul (from Brisbane, Australia) and Annette (from Toulouse, France) tell us about their friendship on different sides of the world.
[00:21.70]When and how did your friendship start?
[00:25.16]Fifteen years ago! We were both fourteen.
[00:29.00]My school in Australia and her school in France started a virtual exchange programme for people to write to each other over the internet.
[00:38.34]I wanted to improve my English and he wanted to improve his French, so we contacted each other.
[00:46.34]Did you become friends immediately?
[00:48.98]Not really.
[00:50.70]I thought he was OK but not special.
[00:55.60]He made lots of mistakes in his French! Haha.
[00:59.24]We corrected each other’s mistakes.
[01:01.82]But I made more than her! She corrected everything, too!
[01:08.68]Paul always helped me to improve my English, but you know, after a few months, we stopped worrying about writing in perfect English or French and just wrote to each other and talked about ourselves.
[01:25.16]I began to see that he was a very nice guy, easy-going and cheerful.
[01:32.22]Did you feel the same way, Paul?
[01:35.06]Yes, I did.
[01:36.52]She often made jokes that made me laugh.
[01:39.06]She’s really funny.
[01:41.38]And we saw that we had a lot of things in common – books we liked, films we liked, things like that.
[01:51.14]He’s also very intelligent.
[01:54.46]So, when did you actually meet face-to-face?
[01:58.88]Last year.
[01:59.66]We met in Singapore.
[02:01.84]That’s right.
[02:02.92]We wanted to meet up, but it’s a long way and the plane tickets are expensive.
[02:08.46]So we decided to meet halfway.
[02:11.34]We had a really great time together for about five days.
[02:15.70]Which language did you speak?
[02:18.28]Haha! Good question.
[02:20.28]English, almost all the time.
[02:23.02]Her English is really good!
[02:25.16]He’s just being kind! But yes, we had a great time.
[02:30.44]I think we’re going to be friends for life.
[02:33.12]I hope we can meet up again soon.
